To find the sum \(\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} f(k)\) where \(f(r) = \sum_{j=2}^{2008} \frac{1}{j^{r}}\), we start by rewriting the double sum: \[
\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} f(k) = \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \sum_{j=2}^{2008} \frac{1}{j^{k}}
\]

We can switch the order of summation: \[
\sum_{j=2}^{2008} \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{j^{k}}
\]

The sum of the geometric series \(\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{j^k}\) is calculated as follows: \[
\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{j^k} = \frac{1/j^2}{1 - 1/j} = \frac{1/j^2}{(j-1)/j} = \frac{1}{j(j-1)}
\]

Thus, the double sum becomes: \[
\sum_{j=2}^{2008} \frac{1}{j(j-1)}
\]

Each term \(\frac{1}{j(j-1)}\) can be written as: \[
\frac{1}{j-1} - \frac{1}{j}
\]

All intermediate terms cancel out, leaving: \[
1 - \frac{1}{2008} = \frac{2007}{2008}
\]

Thus, the final answer is: \[
\boxed{\dfrac{2007}{2008}}
\]
